  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   We are given an initial solution (h_initial) and asked to find a sequence with max_overlap integral C_5 below 0.38085. The goal is to improve current 0.380896. We propose a hybrid strategy combining Bayesian optimization with Gaussian Process kernels to explore the function space. Unlike direct optimization which may get stuck locally, we use Bayesian optimization to generate promising samples at moderate costs. We model the overlap integral as the objective and use a custom score function that balances exploration and exploitation. The Bayesian optimization will take as inputs the sample points and hyperparameters, and rank candidates based on their performance. We provide two fallback strategies: 1) Best solution from Bayesian optimization, and 2) Local search with structured perturbations if budget runs low. This approach addresses local optima by expanding the search space while maintaining performance.\n\n2. Mathematical formulation\n   We seek to minimize:\n      obj(h) = max_k{ \\int_0^2 h(x) [1-h(x+k)] dx } * (2/n_points)\n\n   subject to:\n      h \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n_points},  0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 for all i,\n      \\sum_{i=0}^{n_points-1} h_i = n_points / 2\n\n   We model the optimization problem using a Gaussian Process (GP) with an exponential kernel. The GP is fit to random samples (after self-normalization) to predict the maxOverlap score. The score function is defined as:\n      score(candidate, hp) = min( score_GP(candidate), score_random(candidate) )\n\n   where score_GP uses the GP model and score_random is a lower-fidelity estimate. The GP model is built incrementally, adapting the number of samples based on time. Candidate generation uses:\n      1. Dataset reuse: Start with n_points=280 and transfer parent if needed.\n      2. Multi-level sampling: From large sets to small ones.\n      3. Bayesian ranking: Score by Expected Improvement over historical best.\n\n3. Implementation\n   Run step-by-step:\n   - Phase 0: Validate parent and initialize best\n   - Phase 1: Bayesian optimization loop (max 10 seconds)\n       * Build initial GP with 10 samples (all parents)\n     * Collect score over time (GP + fallback)\n     * Adapt kernel and acquisition function based on memory\n     * Evaluate in batches (1 per 5 seconds)\n   - Phase 2: Leapfrog iterative improvement (if budget remains)\n     * Continuous perturbations: Add random / sine waves\n     * Top-K shifts focus: Apply SLSQP for 1e4 iterations\n   - Final projection: Clip and normalize to meet constraints\n\n   Key helper functions:\n      score(x) -> returns (overlap, time_used)\n      improve(x, hp) -> uses Bayesian tree walk\n      bayes_opt(H, hp) -> bayesian loop\n\n4. Search variation\n   Core Bayesian optimization uses:\n      K = 8 shifts for correlation (adaptive)\n      Exp-probability kernel with lengthscale=0.75\n      Expected Improvement acquisition for improvement over -0.381\n\n   We also fall back to:\n      - Multi-start phase: Uses 8 random samples, each perturbed by normal noise\n      - Local refinement: Projects solutions to meet constraints using convex hull method\n      - Historical archiving: Stores top 8 solutions\n\n5. Budget and fallbacks\n   Total budget: 1100 seconds\n   Safe evaluation points: Normalize samples to feed points\n   Failure handling:\n      * If GP returns NaN, use historical best\n      * If phase 1 takes too long, switch to fine tuning\n      * Budget watch: time.sleep to throttle expensive evaluations\n\n6. Validation and format\n   All candidates are scored using:\n      overlap = np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * (2/n_points)\n   Returns: (n_best, h_final, overlap) with h_final=numpy array\n\n   Checks:\n      - Sum(h_final)==n_points/2 (within 1e-4)\n      - All 0<=h_final<=1\n      - Finite overlap value\n      - Tree search state saved in metric_history",
